WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.981
A California city considers raising downtown parking prices to reduce the time drivers spend circling for a space.

2
00:00:05.981 --> 00:00:07.188
The goal sounds simple.

3
00:00:07.188 --> 00:00:08.447
The response may not be.

4
00:00:08.447 --> 00:00:15.949
Microeconomics studies choices under scarcity, how markets coordinate those choices, and when market outcomes create reasons for public action.

5
00:00:15.949 --> 00:00:17.337
Study preparation only.

6
00:00:17.337 --> 00:00:21.201
This episode does not award credit or determine transfer credit.

7
00:00:21.201 --> 00:00:25.112
Scarcity means available resources cannot satisfy every possible use.

8
00:00:25.112 --> 00:00:28.570
A city has limited curb space, staff time, land, and revenue.

9
00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:33.615
Choosing one use creates an opportunity cost, the value of the best alternative given up.

10
00:00:33.615 --> 00:00:39.737
A curb reserved for parking cannot simultaneously be a bus lane, loading zone, bike lane, or wider sidewalk.

11
00:00:39.737 --> 00:00:44.952
Marginal analysis compares the additional benefit of one more unit with its additional cost.

12
00:00:44.952 --> 00:00:48.410
The question is not whether parking is beneficial in general.

13
00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:53.965
It is whether the next space devoted to parking produces more benefit than the next best curb use.

14
00:00:53.965 --> 00:01:03.035
A rational decision continues while marginal benefit is at least as large as marginal cost, although real people have limited information and may use shortcuts.

15
00:01:03.035 --> 00:01:10.347
Demand describes quantities buyers are willing and able to purchase at different prices, holding other relevant factors constant.

16
00:01:10.347 --> 00:01:12.671
The demand curve usually slopes downward.

17
00:01:12.671 --> 00:01:19.587
At a higher parking price, some drivers shorten visits, use another location, change travel mode, or do not make the trip.

18
00:01:19.587 --> 00:01:24.462
Supply describes quantities sellers are willing and able to offer at different prices.

19
00:01:24.462 --> 00:01:28.713
For curb parking in a fixed downtown, short-run supply may be nearly fixed.

20
00:01:28.713 --> 00:01:30.867
Price cannot quickly create more curb.

21
00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:34.042
It can change who uses existing spaces and for how long.

22
00:01:34.042 --> 00:01:38.293
Market equilibrium occurs where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied.

23
00:01:38.293 --> 00:01:40.334
Equilibrium is not a moral judgment.

24
00:01:40.334 --> 00:01:43.678
It is the point where plans match under current conditions.

25
00:01:43.678 --> 00:01:47.363
A change in price causes movement along a demand or supply curve.

26
00:01:47.363 --> 00:01:49.403
A separate influence shifts a curve.

27
00:01:49.403 --> 00:01:54.562
If a new apartment building brings more residents downtown, parking demand may shift right.

28
00:01:54.562 --> 00:01:58.246
If construction removes a block of spaces, supply may shift left.

29
00:01:58.246 --> 00:02:01.080
Government price controls alter market adjustment.

30
00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:05.615
A binding price ceiling is set below equilibrium and tends to create a shortage.

31
00:02:05.615 --> 00:02:09.980
A binding price floor is set above equilibrium and tends to create a surplus.

32
00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:12.304
The legal price is only part of the cost.

33
00:02:12.304 --> 00:02:18.029
Underpriced parking may produce a queue measured in cruising time, fuel, uncertainty, and congestion.

34
00:02:18.029 --> 00:02:20.637
Efficiency and equity are different questions.

35
00:02:20.637 --> 00:02:27.042
A market may allocate a space to the driver willing to pay most, but willingness to pay depends partly on income.

36
00:02:27.042 --> 00:02:34.411
A city may value access for disabled residents, workers on late shifts, customers, deliveries, and emergency vehicles differently.

37
00:02:34.411 --> 00:02:36.736
Economic analysis can identify tradeoffs.

38
00:02:36.736 --> 00:02:39.173
Public judgment still chooses among values.

39
00:02:39.173 --> 00:02:41.099
Elasticity measures responsiveness.

40
00:02:41.099 --> 00:02:47.425
Price elasticity of demand compares the percentage change in quantity demanded with the percentage change in price.

41
00:02:47.425 --> 00:02:51.276
If quantity changes proportionally more than price, demand is elastic.

42
00:02:51.276 --> 00:02:54.632
If quantity changes proportionally less, demand is inelastic.

43
00:02:54.632 --> 00:03:01.894
Elasticity often varies by time period, available substitutes, share of a person's budget, and whether the purchase feels necessary.

44
00:03:01.894 --> 00:03:05.580
Suppose the city raises the hourly parking price by twenty percent.

45
00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:10.036
If occupied hours fall by thirty percent, demand appears elastic over that range.

46
00:03:10.036 --> 00:03:13.557
If occupied hours fall by two percent, demand appears inelastic.

47
00:03:13.557 --> 00:03:17.243
In the second case, revenue may rise while circling barely changes.

48
00:03:17.243 --> 00:03:21.370
A price can collect money without accomplishing the stated behavioral goal.

49
00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:25.166
Consumers allocate limited income among competing goods and services.

50
00:03:25.166 --> 00:03:29.017
Price changes alter both relative attractiveness and purchasing power.

51
00:03:29.017 --> 00:03:32.428
Habits, framing, and confusing meters can also affect choices.

52
00:03:32.428 --> 00:03:36.499
Taxes and fees create a wedge between what buyers pay and sellers receive.

53
00:03:36.499 --> 00:03:40.185
Statutory incidence identifies who sends payment to the government.

54
00:03:40.185 --> 00:03:45.411
Economic incidence identifies who ultimately bears the burden after prices and behavior adjust.

55
00:03:45.411 --> 00:03:51.188
The less elastic side of a market generally bears more of the burden because it has fewer ways to escape.

56
00:03:51.188 --> 00:03:54.984
If a city taxes garage operators, they may pass some cost to drivers.

57
00:03:54.984 --> 00:03:57.899
Operators absorb more when drivers have alternatives.

58
00:03:57.899 --> 00:04:00.210
Drivers bear more when they cannot adjust.

59
00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:05.602
Any deadweight loss should be compared with benefits from reducing congestion or funding services.

60
00:04:05.602 --> 00:04:07.747
Market structure changes firm behavior.

61
00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:15.339
In perfect competition, many buyers and sellers trade a standardized product, entry is relatively easy, and no single firm controls price.

62
00:04:15.339 --> 00:04:20.786
Competitive pressure tends to push price toward the cost of producing an additional unit over time.

63
00:04:20.786 --> 00:04:24.692
Monopolistic competition has many sellers with differentiated products.

64
00:04:24.692 --> 00:04:28.268
Oligopoly has few major sellers whose choices affect one another.

65
00:04:28.268 --> 00:04:32.119
A monopoly has one seller, barriers to entry, and no close substitute.

66
00:04:32.119 --> 00:04:36.355
It can restrict output and charge more than stronger competition would allow.

67
00:04:36.355 --> 00:04:41.086
Monopoly power can come from legal protection, network effects, or economies of scale.

68
00:04:41.086 --> 00:04:45.983
Local government encounters monopoly questions in utilities, waste hauling, and software.

69
00:04:45.983 --> 00:04:50.439
Contracts should address rates, quality, data portability, performance, and exit.

70
00:04:50.439 --> 00:04:54.675
Regulation, public ownership, and competitive bidding are possible responses.

71
00:04:54.675 --> 00:04:58.494
Markets may fail when prices omit costs or benefits affecting others.

72
00:04:58.494 --> 00:05:01.980
An externality is a spillover on someone outside a transaction.

73
00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:09.396
A driver who circles for parking adds delay and pollution experienced by bus riders, pedestrians, nearby residents, and other drivers.

74
00:05:09.396 --> 00:05:13.989
Those social costs may exceed the private fuel and time costs the driver considers.

75
00:05:13.989 --> 00:05:19.578
A corrective tax or congestion price can make part of an external cost visible to the decision maker.

76
00:05:19.578 --> 00:05:21.515
Regulation can set limits directly.

77
00:05:21.515 --> 00:05:25.168
Tradable permits can cap a total quantity while allowing exchange.

78
00:05:25.168 --> 00:05:28.267
Each instrument requires measurement and administration.

79
00:05:28.267 --> 00:05:32.418
Setting the perfect price is difficult when damages vary by place and time.

80
00:05:32.418 --> 00:05:34.853
A public good is nonrival and nonexcludable.

81
00:05:34.853 --> 00:05:38.837
Nonrival means one person's use does not substantially reduce another's.

82
00:05:38.837 --> 00:05:42.545
Nonexcludable means keeping nonpayers from benefiting is difficult.

83
00:05:42.545 --> 00:05:47.415
Emergency warning information and some forms of basic scientific knowledge are examples.

84
00:05:47.415 --> 00:05:51.565
Private markets may underprovide public goods because people can free ride.

85
00:05:51.565 --> 00:05:53.281
Common resources are different.

86
00:05:53.281 --> 00:05:57.985
They are difficult to exclude people from, but one person's use reduces what remains.

87
00:05:57.985 --> 00:05:59.867
A fishery is the standard example.

88
00:05:59.867 --> 00:06:02.523
Congested public streets can share this feature.

89
00:06:02.523 --> 00:06:08.444
Street parking is rival because one vehicle occupies a space, and meters or permits can make it excludable.

90
00:06:08.444 --> 00:06:12.263
Public ownership does not automatically make something a public good.

91
00:06:12.263 --> 00:06:14.421
Apply the analysis to downtown parking.

92
00:06:14.421 --> 00:06:19.236
Begin with a measurable objective, such as one available space per block at peak hours.

93
00:06:19.236 --> 00:06:23.165
Collect occupancy, turnover, circling, transit delay, and revenue data.

94
00:06:23.165 --> 00:06:25.655
Test elasticity in a limited area and period.

95
00:06:25.655 --> 00:06:29.861
A late-shift worker may respond differently from a shopper with substitutes.

96
00:06:29.861 --> 00:06:31.964
Equity protections should be explicit.

97
00:06:31.964 --> 00:06:37.997
Options include an overnight worker permit, accessible spaces, improved loading rules, or transit investment.

98
00:06:37.997 --> 00:06:40.930
A permit that is too broad can recreate the shortage.

99
00:06:40.930 --> 00:06:44.582
Finally, name the externality and the market structure accurately.

100
00:06:44.582 --> 00:06:46.353
Circling creates external costs.

101
00:06:46.353 --> 00:06:48.345
The curb itself is scarce and rival.

102
00:06:48.345 --> 00:06:52.607
A contracted parking-app vendor may have market power if switching is costly.

103
00:06:52.607 --> 00:06:56.093
Clear labels lead to narrower remedies and better measurements.

104
00:06:56.093 --> 00:06:57.626
Three recall questions.

105
00:06:57.626 --> 00:07:01.760
First, why does elasticity matter when a city changes a price?

106
00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:03.827
Second, who bears a tax or fee?

107
00:07:03.827 --> 00:07:07.427
Third, how do an externality and a public good differ?

108
00:07:07.427 --> 00:07:15.623
First answer: elasticity indicates whether a price change is likely to cause a large behavioral response or mostly change payment and revenue.

109
00:07:15.623 --> 00:07:22.839
Second answer: the legal payer sends the money, but the economic burden is shared according to how buyers and sellers adjust.

110
00:07:22.839 --> 00:07:25.378
The less responsive side usually bears more.

111
00:07:25.378 --> 00:07:29.477
Third answer: an externality is a cost or benefit imposed on outsiders.

112
00:07:29.477 --> 00:07:35.422
A public good is both nonrival and nonexcludable, which can lead private markets to provide too little.

113
00:07:35.422 --> 00:07:40.156
Artifact: write a two-hundred-fifty-word California city parking memo.

114
00:07:40.156 --> 00:07:51.925
State the scarce resource and opportunity cost, the externality, your elasticity hypothesis, expected tax or fee incidence, one equity concern, and one sixty-day measurement.

115
00:07:51.925 --> 00:07:57.944
Add one sentence explaining why the curb is not a public good and one alternative policy.

116
00:07:57.944 --> 00:08:27.635
Source note: This original study script draws on OpenStax, Principles of Microeconomics 3e, especially Chapters 1 through 6 on economic choice, tradeoffs, supply and demand, price controls, elasticity, and consumer choice; Chapter 10, Monopolistic Competition and Oligopoly; Chapter 11, Monopoly and Antitrust Policy; Chapter 12, Environmental Protection and Negative Externalities; and Chapter 13, Positive Externalities and Public Goods.

